[Music]  [Applause]  welcome to Chicago the Windy City in the  sight of some of the greatest modern  architecture in the world today we're  here to talk about Ludwig Mies van der  Rohe a founding father of modernism and  a towering giant in architectural  history in his work there is a classical  sense of proportion scale and order but  without any ornament decoration or  flourishes using materials like glass  steel or marble his buildings are strong  they're muscular they're brutal and they  have never been more revered than they  are today  this is crown Hall the architecture  school at the Illinois Institute of  Technology mace used to be the director  here and some say it is his greatest  building a twenty six thousand square  feet from old seems to float above the  lawn it has four huge trusses that are  reachable by the waterfall of marble  steps the main floor of this masterpiece  is a large and column-free classroom it  features a suspended roof in a totally  open floor plan that can be adapted to  any use at all when you're in here you  start to understand the concept the  meaning of universal space which is a  term coined by Mies van der Rohe to  describe the flexibility of the spaces  how you can transform or adapt or model  a specific space into whatever  requirement you need and until this day  the students come here they use this  space and they follow the curriculum  that means creative so this space is  alive you see the lines you see the  severity but it's actually the opposite  to severity it has to do with  flexibility  as you may notice this small building is  a little bit different it is a cube  instead of a big pavilion it has brick  walls rather than steel frame it's  called the car memorial chapel but  students around here call it the God box  [Music]  this chapel was built in 1952 and it is  actually the only ecclesiastical space  ever designed by Mies van der Rohe it  bursts almost no resemblance to a  Renaissance or Gothic cathedral instead  it is a temple to God into modernism  inside the guard Box remains stark and  simple this is Miss wanted it steel  railings a marble altar a curtain and of  course across services are still held  here every Sunday  Mies once said too often we think about  architecture in terms of the spectacular  there is nothing spectacular about this  chapel it was not meant to be  spectacular it was meant to be simple  and in fact it is simple but in its  simplicity it is not primitive but noble  and in its smallness it is great in fact  monumental  [Music]  one of the things that I love the most  about Miss Vonda Rose work here at IIT  if there is a school it's not a museum  it's not a shrine it's a place where  people come to learn to study to become  the next generation of architects that  may go off and change the world with  what they build miss van der rote has  buried a few miles from here  at Chicago's Graceland Cemetery and I  really hope that makes him proud  [Music]

